        "On a hot afternoon in July, the surface of a dark roof can become hot enough to fry an egg. The air in the rooms below grows warm as well, and people switch on their air conditioners, which push even more heat out into the streets. Researchers call this the “urban heat island” effect: a city is often several degrees warmer than the countryside around it, especially at night.",
