        "The results were clear. On sunny days, the white roofs were on average 22 degrees cooler at the surface than the dark ones, and the top-floor rooms beneath them were about 3 degrees cooler in the late afternoon. Families living under the white roofs ran their air conditioners for roughly a third less time. “Three degrees may not sound like much,” Ferreira said, “but for an elderly person living alone, it can be the difference between an uncomfortable night and a dangerous one.”",
        "The team also found some limits. In winter, the white roofs reflected sunlight that would otherwise have helped to warm the buildings, so heating bills rose slightly. The paint also collected dirt, and after three years it reflected about 15 percent less light unless it was washed. Ferreira believes white roofs make the most sense in places with long, hot summers and mild winters.",
